<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polyperchon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 394–c. 303 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Polyperchon was a Macedonian general who served under Philip II and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. In 319 BCE the dying regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antipater">Antipater</a> named him guardian of the kings in preference to his own son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassander">Cassander</a>, setting off a civil war among Alexander's successors. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a>, he lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> to Cassander by 316 BCE and later held out for years in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p></div>
